00AFEB0, fetchcam #332), then wedged in LegacyCdSectorFetch (fn 0x80042AA8) 50s+ to the fuse, ALIVE, polling status bit 0x40 with the drive idle - R967 STUCK x4 (FDF8=800 A22C=1 flag578A6=0 identical across 30s), mvdoor-near x6, alarmguard x6, all cur_fn=80042AA8. The R1246 c103 comment named the class: the fetch PsyQ-table command sequence never engages our read machinery, the drive stays idle, and the 0x40 bit (computed from cd_read_active && data-in-FIFO) can NEVER set. NO existing door covers it (zrf0/zrfY need FDF8==0, R1267 needs FDF8==0, R96 needs cmd 06/09+read_active, mvdoor needs cmd 02/06 and is on_alarm-starved in spins, C97). THE FIX: door at case 0x1F801800 - the status read the fetch itself polls (the hottest spin-reached site, 14.4M+ polls receipted) - gated on the EXACT receipted posture (act=0 pend=0 cmd=01 seek<150 FE04=0 FDF8==2048) with 65536-poll stuck confirmation; action = the c139-141 secserve composite: same-poll cd_data_load + cd_read_active=1 so the 0x40 bit computes true, the fetch own DMA drains the sector, FDF8 2048 -> 0, the fetch completes natively. No INT forge, no guest dispatch (R350-safe). Budget 12. PASS = fetch consumes, FDF8 drives to 0, forward execution past 80042AA8 (new state-table activity, first non-boot GPU activity). REVERT if the fetch consumes but the game does not advance. SHIPPING NOTE c181->c182: the c181 patch placed this door INSIDE the three-line status return expression - the Mac compile refused (expected ; after return statement), run.sh correctly refused a stale binary, zero R1282 evidence. c182 places the door BEFORE the complete return statement and sandbox-PARSES the patched file (gcc -fsyntax-only against the baseline error set) before shipping. Base R1281 parked (zero fires in c178, kept in tree): the ONE failing gate term of the c348-class stuck-read arm, receipted by the c176 census, removed. THE c176 DECODE: the c175 state-1 line was NOT game progress - it was the game own REBOOT pass (bootmain entered, epoch=1, garbage cells mid-boot) after the file#3 spin failed, and exit 99 was the crash-kit converting a confirmed LZSS runaway (op src=0x00000000, 1M+ suppressed reads, frontier marching 0x8028F358..0x805FF358 - the c335 class; serve the file and neither happens). The real blocker, receipted end-to-end: at t~50s the game armed file#3 (seek=108785, FDF8=155120 = exact ftab size, dst=8007F2F8) via its own stab chain - read_act=1, loaded=0, cmd 06 - and the R1163 sgarm gate declined it because cd_scheduled=1 (the sgdecline first-eval #3 receipted EVERY gate input true EXCEPT scheduled; stale or fresh-queued from the movie/state-6 teardown - either way the serve completes the request and the proven R541 fldfin end-of-read chain clears scheduled at FDF8=0). The game spun 91M polls at the archive loader 800286CC, gave up at t=85s, rebooted, and the reboot LZSS runaway took it down via R1151 crash-kit recovery. THE FIX: remove the scheduled==0 term from the sgarm outer gate - the arm still requires the shape to persist across TWO 2s observations with FDF8 UNCHANGED (a live scheduler serve would be decreasing FDF8), all other terms still gate, the 96-delivery burst still checks FDF8 progress, and the receipt now prints scheduled. PASS = sgarm fires with scheduled=1, FDF8 drives to 0, the archive walk resumes (file#3 then onward - the boot-era chain receipts the stab/stepper path works), the game does NOT reach the reboot, exit code not 99. REVERT if sgarm fires and the archive walk does not resume. Base R1280 = zrfB A22C-site system-band serve (12+ fires, PASSED). Base R1275 = the state-6 heap seed (TRAP=0 ABRT=0).\n" );
    { /* R228: SIGALTSTACK. Cycle-14 verdict: the R227 handler was correctly
         * installed (clean compile, banner R227) and STILL printed nothing —
         * the remaining failure mode is STACK EXHAUSTION: the movie-player
         * chain appears stuck in a call loop, the native stack blows in ~2s,
         * and a dead stack cannot deliver the signal frame to any handler
         * running on that stack. SA_ONSTACK + a dedicated alternate stack lets
         * the report run anyway — and the 32-fn call trail will show the
         * repeating function = the loop's name. */
        static char crash_stack[1024 * 1024]; /* R1130 (b8-c311): 64KB->1MB.
         * c311 exit-139 ips: KERN_PROTECTION_FAILURE, rip==cr2 on a HOST
         * STACK page, "invalid protections for user instruction read",
         * faulting thread running our runtime (r14=&xenolift_sw_line) with
         * guest addrs in host regs - the nested-signal + guest-exception-
         * route chain (R772 route runs guest code INSIDE the handler; c311
         * [exithv] receipted a sig=10 IN-HANDLER FAULT @0x8004C2F0 during
         * exactly that route) exhausted the shared 64KB altstack. The guest
         * route needs our full C-recursive executor on that stack. 1MB. */
        stack_t ss;
        /* R247: host stack bounds for the crash walker — fp chain copies
         * are only safe INSIDE the real thread stack. A garbage-but-aligned
         * rbp previously faulted INSIDE the SIGSEGV handler -> nested fault
         * on the altstack -> hang (cycle-1 of the 500 marathon). */
        {
            volatile char probe;
            host_stack_hi = (uint64_t)(uintptr_t)&probe;
            host_stack_lo = host_stack_hi - XENOLIFT_GUEST_STACK; /* R261: matches the guest thread stack */
        }
        struct sigaction sa;
        memset(crash_stack, 0, sizeof crash_stack);
        ss.ss_sp = crash_stack;
        ss.ss_size = sizeof crash_stack;
        ss.ss_flags = 0;
        sigaltstack(&ss, NULL);
        memset(&sa, 0, sizeof(sa));
        sa.sa_sigaction = segv_handler;
        sa.sa_flags = SA_SIGINFO | SA_ONSTACK;
        sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
        sigaction(SIGSEGV, &sa, NULL);
        sigaction(SIGBUS, &sa, NULL);
    }
    if (argc < 2) {
        r861_out("usage: ./xenogears_boot <psx-exe> [disc-image]\n");
        return 1;
    }
    load_exe(argv[1]);
    bios_module_setup();
    hle_gte_reset();    /* R136: GTE owns COP2 state */
    gpu_init();         /* R135: GPU HLE interpreter */
    hle_spu_reset();    /* R134: SPU HLE module */
    hle_mdec_reset();   /* R134: MDEC HLE module */
    hle_memcard_reset();/* R134: memcard HLE (SIO routing deferred) */
    pad_fill(); /* R85: connected digital pad before boot */
    /* R87: state-machine latch (gp+448 = 0x80059330). The walker
     * fn_800320F8 (called from the display step's report at
     * 0x80019ED4) traps 0x83 when latch==0; the alloc gatekeeper
     * fn_80031DB0 also NULLs decompression-class allocs when it is 0.
     * On real HW the state-machine install (set_latch at 0x80031BB8)
     * runs BEFORE the display step, so arming at init matches the real
     * ordering. Both readers only test != 0 — a flag value is safe. */
    { uint32_t latch = 1; memcpy(xenolift_mem + 0x59330u, &latch, 4);
      r861_out("[latch] state-machine latch armed (0x80059330 = 1)\n"); }
    {
        static int once; if (!once) { once = 1;
            r861_out("[pad] connected digital pad armed (0x800625FC pair)\n"); }
    }
    /* kernel DMA cell pointers -> register addresses. The kernel arms
     * ch3 (CDROM) through cells: MADR/BCR/CHCR at 0x800567AC/B0/B4, DPCR/
     * DICR at 0x800567A4/A8, RAM_SIZE cell at 0x80056780. */
    {
        static const struct { uint32_t cell, val; } cd_cells[] = {
            { 0x80056780u, 0x1F801020u },
            { 0x800567A4u, 0x1F8010F0u },
            { 0x800567A8u, 0x1F8010F4u },
            { 0x800567ACu, 0x1F8010B0u },
            { 0x800567B0u, 0x1F8010B4u },
            { 0x800567B4u, 0x1F8010B8u },
        };
        for (unsigned i = 0; i < sizeof(cd_cells) / sizeof(cd_cells[0]); i++)
            *(uint32_t *)(xenolift_mem + (cd_cells[i].cell & 0x1FFFFFFFu)) = cd_cells[i].val;
        r861_out("[cells] CD/DMA register cells initialized\n");
        /* R154: THE WORKING GAME TABLE. Live proof (cycle 5): the kernel
         * commits the requested state to the ROM/idx cell 0x80018088, but
         * the resident-loop dispatcher reads state from 0x80018088 and
         * descriptors from 0x8001808C - a working copy that NOTHING ever
         * initializes (raw disc code sits there). Every cycle it computed a
         * wild descriptor pointer and aborted to the restart step before
         * the entry dispatch. Retail hardware must build this copy before
         * boot; our runtime performs the same install here: 7 entries
         * copied from the verified ROM table 0x8001808C, working idx
         * mirrored from 0x80018088.
         *
         * R1232 (c73, THE IDX-MIRROR FIX): ground truth from the pristine
         * exe disassembly (SLUS_006.64, tools/mips decode) - the phase
         * dispatcher fn_80019ACC reads its working index from 0x80028088
         * (lui 0x8002 + lw 0x8088), NOT 0x80018088: s1 = *(0x80028088) +
         * 0x8002808C = row pointer, so the working cell holds the SCALED
         * byte offset idx*16 (row 6 = base + 0x60 = 0x800280EC, exactly
         * where the working table's state-6 pFnMain 0x800737EC is
         * installed - hook receipt 0x800280EC). c73 receipts: req=6 sat in
         * the ROM cell all era, NO writer ever touched 0x80028088 (empty
         * 5FAEC/28088 write sections), and the OLD mirror was a LITERAL
         * NO-OP - it wrote rom_idx BACK to 0x80018088, the cell it just
         * read from - so the dispatcher resolved row 0 and dispatched the
         * menu forever while the state-6 request went unconsumed (R670
         * phase receipts: cb resolved via camera-side table lookup, but
         * active state stayed 0, phase=-1, cur=6, req=6). The mount state:
         * MountGameStateModule(6) ran, carve took file#18 (14360), F0C=18
         * FDF8=0 FE1C=0 seek=109166 - everything completed except the
         * dispatch. Fix: write the SCALED idx (rom_idx*16, masked to the
         * 8-row table) to 0x80028088. Same install class as the existing
         * table copy - completing it, not adding new scaffolding. */
        memcpy(xenolift_mem + 0x2808Cu, xenolift_mem + 0x1808Cu, 7u * 16u);
        {
            uint32_t rom_idx = 0;
            memcpy(&rom_idx, xenolift_mem + 0x18088u, 4);
            uint32_t wk_idx = (rom_idx & 7u) * 16u;
            memcpy(xenolift_mem + 0x28088u, &wk_idx, 4);
            r861_out("[gtab-init] R1232 working game table installed: 7 descriptors, rom-idx=0x%08X -> working-idx(0x80028088)=0x%08X (scaled idx*16 - dispatcher fn_80019ACC reads THIS cell; c73: old mirror was a no-op write-back to 0x18088)\n", rom_idx, wk_idx);
        }
    }
    if (argc > 2 && xenolift_disc_mount(argv[2]) != 0) {
        r861_out("[disc] continuing without a disc (file functions fail)\n");
    }

    /* watchdog: 15 seconds of emulated execution is plenty for a smoke test */
    /* R914 (b8-c15, WARDEN lane - ALARM-HANDLER DEDICATED STACK, the
     * named canary cure): c15's R853 resolution + R909 ctx name the
     * six-cycle canary killer: __xvprintf's OWN canary smashed mid-print
     * on the MAIN thread (depth=1, cur_fn=80019524) with the resolved
     * smashed-fn = cd_sched_poll_release (an on_alarm drain path,
     * defib5). Mechanism: SIGALRM is process-directed; when it lands
     * while the anchor thread is INSIDE r861_out/vsnprintf, the handler
     * runs ON MAIN'S STACK, its drain paths call r861_out again, and the
     * interleaved printf frames corrupt __xvprintf's frame -> canary ->
     * SIGABRT (exit 134). Same family as c480's torn CD read (R885) but
     * on the PRINT path - the depth guard can't see it. STRUCTURAL FIX:
     * give the handler its own stack (sigaltstack + SA_ONSTACK) so
     * handler frames NEVER interleave with main's printf frames. The
     * guest machinery keeps its own guards. */
    { static char r914_alstack[64 * 1024]; /* dedicated, never freed */
      stack_t r914_ss;
      r914_ss.ss_sp = r914_alstack;
      r914_ss.ss_size = sizeof r914_alstack;
      r914_ss.ss_flags = 0;
      if (sigaltstack(&r914_ss, NULL) != 0) {
          r861_out("[r914] WARN: sigaltstack failed - alarm canary risk stays\n");
      }
      struct sigaction r914_sa;
      r914_sa.sa_handler = NULL; /* R1199: SA_SIGINFO form - the handler now receives the signal context */
      r914_sa.sa_sigaction = on_alarm_ctx;
      sigemptyset(&r914_sa.sa_mask);
      r914_sa.sa_flags = SA_RESTART | SA_ONSTACK | SA_SIGINFO;
      if (sigaction(SIGALRM, &r914_sa, NULL) != 0) {
          r861_out("[r914] WARN: SIGALRM sigaction failed - falling back to signal()\n");
          signal(SIGALRM, on_alarm_plain);
      } else {
          r861_out("[r914] alarm handler on dedicated stack (sigaltstack %zuKB + SA_ONSTACK; R1130 upsized the segv altstack 64KB->1MB after the c311 exit-139 class) - printf-frame interleave class dead\n", sizeof r914_alstack / 1024);
      } }
    alarm(30); /* R305: 30s initial window — state-1 mount era */

    /* mirror the console's initial CPU state */
    r[0] = 0; /* hardwired zero (and never written by our generated code) */
    r[31] = 0;
    hi = lo = 0;

    xenolift_region(); /* run the recompiled boot code */

    /* R678: SHELL RESTART — c246-c249 converged structural read: the kernel
     * menu's design is pick-state -> main RETURNS -> the shell re-enters the
     * exe with that state. Our shell just exits, so the field era dies at
     * the first clean return (@10-11s, cur=1 committed, state-1 cb entered).
     * Restart the boot chain like the console shell would. The boot's own
     * BSS clear (fn_80019524) resets the kernel cells natively; receipts from
     * the second boot tell us what the restart needs next. Cap 2. */
    {
        static uint32_t r678_n;
        while (r678_n < 2u &&
               (xenolift_mem_read32(0x800592C0u) == 1u ||
                xenolift_mem_read32(0x8005FAECu) >= 1u)) {
            r678_n++;
            r861_out("[mtrans] R678 shell RESTART #%u @t=%lds: main returned with field committed (92C0=%08X FAEC=%08X) - re-entering boot like the console shell would\n",
                    r678_n, (long)(xl_wall() - g_boot_wall_t0),
                    xenolift_mem_read32(0x800592C0u), xenolift_mem_read32(0x8005FAECu));
            r[31] = 0; hi = lo = 0;
            xenolift_boot_epoch++; g_in_park = 0; g_park_stage = 0; /* R1213: park latch reset at program re-launch (c37 relay-escapee fix) */
            g_menu_seen = 0u; g_menu_press_state = 0u; g_menu_frames = 0u;
            xenolift_region();
        }
    }
    r861_out("[xenolift] region returned (the boot chain unwound).\n");
    return 0;
}

/* R258: deliver a stuck CD INT1 from ANY execution context. Mirrors the
 * proven R159/R160 spin-conv sequence (handler pair + collector) with full
 * register-file + hi/lo save/restore and a reentrancy guard. */
static void cd_force_deliver_int1(const char *why)
{
    static int busy; static uint32_t n;
    if (busy) return;
    /* R898 (c494, manager): the canary is NAMED — this host fn's frame is
     * smashed when called from the WATCHER thread while the anchor is
     * mid-guest-dispatch (c494: cur_fn=80077E88 coordinator on anchor,
     * canary on tid 140704468798912 = watcher; c474: same shape in
     * cd_sched_poll_release). Class = the R350/race family: two threads
     * running guest dispatches concurrently tear the shared dispatch
     * state and a wild write lands on the watcher's stack. FIX (the
     * alarmguard R885 precedent): NEVER dispatch guest handlers from
     * the watcher while the anchor is inside a guest step — set the
     * defer flag and return; the churn anchor drains it between
     * dispatches, where the boot loop crosses boundaries constantly. */
    if (!pthread_equal(pthread_self(), xenolift_churn_tid) && g_guest_depth > 0) {
        static int r898_n;
        g_cdf_defer = 1u;
        if (r898_n++ < 6)
            r861_out("[cdf] R898 force-deliver DEFERRED (%s) - watcher mid-anchor-step (depth=%d cur_fn=%08X) - anchor drains at next boundary\n",
                    why, g_guest_depth, xenolift_cur_fn);
        return;
    }
    busy = 1;
    {
        uint32_t sr[32]; uint32_t shi = hi, slo = lo;
        memcpy(sr, r, sizeof r);
        n++;
        if (n <= 16u || (n % 64u) == 0u)
            r861_out("[cdf] force-deliver #%u (%s): INT1 pending=%u seek=%u FDF8=%u — handler pair + collector\n",
                    n, why, cd_pending, cd_seek_lba, xenolift_mem_read32(0x8004FDF8u));
        r[4] = 0; r[5] = 0;
        if (!r1179_forge_blocked("L21546")) { g_guest_depth++, xenolift_dispatch(0x800409E4u), g_guest_depth--; }
        if (!r1179_forge_blocked("L21547")) { g_guest_depth++, xenolift_dispatch(0x80040A4Cu), g_guest_depth--; }
        if (!r1179_forge_blocked("L21548")) { g_guest_depth++, xenolift_dispatch(0x800415B4u), g_guest_depth--; }
        {
            /* R259: the collector RETURNS the slot bitmask in v0 — the kernel
             * wait-loop then dispatches h4 (data reader) / h2 (starter). R258
             * dropped it: cycle-7 = 8 sectors got through via stray kernel
             * contexts, then the read parked with the sector LOADED, INT
             * consumed, data_pos=0 (nobody read it), FDF8=6684. Mirror the
             * wait-loop's dispatch exactly (same cells, fallbacks, args). */
            uint32_t slots = r[2];
            if (slots & 4u) {
                uint32_t h4 = *(uint32_t *)(xenolift_mem + 0x564AC);
                /* R1181 (c372): NO FORGED READY-CALLBACK. c372 receipts (firstfault.pSYW6z, park onset
                 * after L287291): "[cd] wl: h4 cell + read-struct both empty - known-native h4 0x8002B084"
                 * -> "wait-loop: dispatch h4 0x8002B084 (a0=0)" -> host backtrace: getintr(800415B4) ->
                 * xenolift_trace -> ArchiveCurrentFileReadyCallback(a0=0) -> CdControlF -> CD_cw ->
                 * CD_sync loop (80041BA8/80041C64, VSync-timed, Alarm 0x8005A228) parked 21s until the
                 * R845 re-force door. The game had NO ready callback registered (cell 0x800564AC empty,
                 * read-struct empty) - it never asked for this call. Under first-fault-stop an empty cell
                 * means nothing is dispatched; the game's own getintr polling owns the INT. */
                if (g_firstfault_stop && !(h4 >= 0x80010000u && h4 < 0x80060000u)) {
                    static int r1181_n;
                    if (r1181_n < 12) { r1181_n++;
                        xenolift_receipt("[firstfault] R1181 h4 cell empty (%08X, read-struct %08X) - NO fallback callback dispatched (slot=%u pending=%u last_cmd=%02X Intr.sync=%02X Intr.ready=%02X)\n",
                                         h4, xenolift_mem_read32(0x80059EF8u + 0x10u), slots, (unsigned)cd_pending,
                                         cd_last_cmd, xenolift_mem[0x56788], xenolift_mem[0x56789]); }
                    h4 = 0u;
                } else if (!(h4 >= 0x80010000u && h4 < 0x80060000u)) {
                    uint32_t rs_h4 = xenolift_mem_read32(0x80059EF8u + 0x10u);
                    h4 = (rs_h4 >= 0x80010000u && rs_h4 < 0x80060000u) ? rs_h4 : 0x8002B084u;
                }
                if (h4 != 0u) { /* R1182: R1181 zeroed h4 but this site dispatched unconditionally -> dispatch(0) x12 in c373 */
                    r[4] = xenolift_mem[0x56789]; r[5] = 0x8005A218u;
                    g_guest_depth++, xenolift_dispatch(h4), g_guest_depth--;
                }
            }
            if (slots & 2u) {
                uint32_t h2 = *(uint32_t *)(xenolift_mem + 0x564A8);
                if (h2 >= 0x80010000u && h2 < 0x80060000u) {
                    r[4] = xenolift_mem[0x56788]; r[5] = 0x8005A210u;
                    g_guest_depth++, xenolift_dispatch(h2), g_guest_depth--;
                }
            }
            if (n <= 16u || (n % 64u) == 0u)
                r861_out("[cdf] #%u post: slots=%u pending=%u seek=%u FDF8=%u fifo=%u/%u loaded=%u\n",
                        n, slots, cd_pending, cd_seek_lba, xenolift_mem_read32(0x8004FDF8u),
                        cd_data_pos, cd_data_n, cd_data_loaded);
        }
        memcpy(r, sr, sizeof r); hi = shi; lo = slo;
    }
    busy = 0;
}

/* R308: central restore — the pending multi-byte answer must reach the handler
 * intact no matter WHICH delivery door the kernel collects it at. Hardware
 * delivers the INT within interrupt latency; our lazy INT lets GetStat polls
 * stomp the FIFO between build and delivery. Restore at every door. */
static void cd_restore_pend(void)
{
    if (cd_pend_ans_n > 0u) {
        memcpy(cd_resp, cd_pend_ans, sizeof cd_resp);
        cd_resp_n = cd_pend_ans_n; cd_resp_pos = 0;
        memcpy(cd_last_full, cd_pend_ans, sizeof cd_resp);
        cd_last_full_n = cd_pend_ans_n;
        { static uint32_t tocr_n; /* R1271: the fd-tick era restores up to 4x/round - the c157 raw log hit 35.5MB and the R751 logcap ate the middle. First 40 + every 500th. */
          tocr_n++;
          if (tocr_n <= 40u || (tocr_n % 500u) == 0u)
            r861_out("[cd] TOC-RESTORE(conv): full %u-byte answer restored (cmd 0x%02X)\n", cd_pend_ans_n, cd_pend_ans_cmd); }
        { extern int g_rspop_win; g_rspop_win = 24; } /* R317 */
    }
}

/* R790 SHARED FIELD-MODULE EXPANSION (c373): the expansion sequence
 * (R703 window-0 + R708 disc-read + R705 stage-2 + R734/R735 window-0
 * overwrite + R707 terminator) previously fired ONLY at the coordinator
 * dispatch (a==0x80077E88). c373 took the SIMULATED-mount trajectory:
 * the phase-hook completed the f15 install WITHOUT the native walk, so
 * neither expansion ran - window-0 kept its half-installed state and the
 * module never executed. Both paths must converge on an expanded module:
 * callable from the coordinator-entry hook AND the phase-hook bulk
 * completion. One-shot statics are shared = expansion happens once per
 * process, whichever trajectory arrives first. */
static void xenolift_field_expand_all(const char *via)
{
    r861_out("[fldx] R790 expansion sequence entered (via %s; coordinator slot %08X %08X; R1002 staged f14@801D9724=%08X gate=%s f15@801F8724=%08X)\n", via,
            xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u), xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 4u),
            xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D9724u),
            ((xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D9724u) & 0x00FFFFFFu) == 0x0003FAFEu) ? "MATCH" : "MISS",
            xenolift_mem_read32(0x801F8724u));
    /* R1003 (b8-c117): c117 named the decline - the staged f14 slot is ZERO
     * because this trajectory never stages there; the compressed module
     * streams straight INTO the module window instead (coordw: fn 800286CC
     * ArchiveDataSync t=14s; ATLAS delivered file#3's 155120B into
     * 0x8007F2F8; coordinator dispatched @t=63s on a ZEROED entry after
     * the t=32s pristine restore). Dump every plausible LZSS-header
     * candidate IN the module window at expansion time so the retarget
     * gate can be written from a receipt next cycle: the LZSS header shape
     * (00xxxx3FAFE per R703/R708) is source-proven from the game's own
     * UnpackCompressedBuffer. */
    {
        static int mh_n;
        if (mh_n < 6) {
            mh_n++;
            r861_out("[modhdr] R1003 module-window LZSS-header candidates @t=%lds:\n",
                    (long)(xl_wall() - g_boot_wall_t0));
            static const struct { uint32_t a; const char *n; } _cand[] = {
                {0x8006F000u, "winbase"}, {0x8006FAF8u, "modbase"}, {0x8007F2F8u, "file3dst"},
                {0x8007EBF0u, "stage2ref"}, {0x801D9724u, "stagedf14"}, {0x801F8724u, "stagedf15"},
            };
            for (unsigned ci = 0; ci < sizeof _cand / sizeof _cand[0]; ci++)
                r861_out("[modhdr]   %s @%08X: %08X %08X %08X %08X\n", _cand[ci].n, _cand[ci].a,
                        xenolift_mem_read32(_cand[ci].a), xenolift_mem_read32(_cand[ci].a + 4u),
                        xenolift_mem_read32(_cand[ci].a + 8u), xenolift_mem_read32(_cand[ci].a + 12u));
        }
    }
                  {   /* R703 [fldreloc] c274 PROOF: the kernel NATIVELY called the
                       * field coordinator (idx=1 cb=80077E88, first time ever) but its
                       * code window 0x8006F000-0x80090000 is still the pre-install
                       * garbage island (executed words 12000044/F00E1220 = non-code,
                       * wild jump 0xF8C43C01). The staged module at 0x801D9724 (f14,
                       * 125304B, header size 0x3FAFE=260862) is the REAL field code,
                       * compressed; the game's own expansion (LZSS -> window-0 slot
                       * 0x8006FAF0) runs only on the shell-restart second boot, which
                       * never gets the chance (era dies at the first coordinator call).
                       * Expand it HERE, at the entry hook BEFORE the first coordinator
                       * instruction executes, using our proven HLE - the kernel then
                       * runs real field code natively. Gates: staged fingerprint
                       * (0x0003FAFE header), coordinator slot still garbage, one-shot. */
                      static int r703n;
                      uint32_t _stg = xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D9724u);
                      uint32_t _co = xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u);
                      if (r703n < 1
                          && (_stg & 0x00FFFFFFu) == 0x0003FAFEu
                          && _co != 0u
                          && xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 4u) != 0u) {
                          uint32_t _w0 = _stg & 0x003FFFFFu;
                          /* R1122: probe BEFORE committing - a fingerprint
                           * match alone is not proof the stream is the
                           * module (c119 winscan); only the proven prologue
                           * unlocks the window write. */
                          uint32_t _pw[4] = {0, 0, 0, 0};
                          int _pok = xenolift_lzss_probe(0x801D9724u, 0x8006FAF0u, _w0, _pw);
                          static int r1122_n;
                          if (!(_pok > 0 && _pw[0] == 0x27BDFFC8u && _pw[1] == 0x3C038001u
                                && _pw[2] == 0x8C630000u && _pw[3] == 0x2402FFFFu)) {
                              if (r1122_n < 6) {
                                  r1122_n++;
                                  r861_out("[fldgate] R1122 expansion DECLINED: staged stream decodes to %08X %08X %08X %08X (probe rc=%d) - not FieldMain prologue; live translated window preserved\n",
                                          _pw[0], _pw[1], _pw[2], _pw[3], _pok);
                              }
                          } else {
                          int _pr = xenolift_lzss_hle(0x801D9724u, 0x8006FAF0u, _w0);
                          r703n++;
                          r861_out("[fldreloc] R703 field module EXPANDED into window-0 at coordinator entry: staged@801D9724 -> 0x8006FAF0, %d of %u words, coordinator slot now: %08X %08X %08X %08X\n",
                                  _pr, _w0,
                                  xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u),
                                  xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 4u),
                                  xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 8u),
                                  xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 12u));
                          }
                      }
                  }
                  {   /* R1005 (b8-c119, AREA 1 - the RETARGET FIX). c119's
                       * winscan is decisive: hits=0 at EVERY burst-done -
                       * the data streaming into the module window carries
                       * NO LZSS header anywhere (0x8006F000-0x8FF000 swept
                       * while coord=EBE10222..EEBC0235 was landing) - the
                       * current trajectory delivers a NON-module asset
                       * (file#3, 155120B) into the window; the real module
                       * (f14, 125304B, LZSS, header 0x0003FAFE - R703/R708
                       * receipts) is a file request this trajectory never
                       * reaches. The staged-f14 gate can therefore never
                       * match. RETARGET: fetch file#14 straight from the
                       * disc ourselves at the coordinator-entry expansion
                       * point - ftab layout is receipt-proven ([ftab]:
                       * entries at 0x800100AC + 7*(n-2), LBA u24 LE +
                       * size u24 LE; file#2..7 decoded every cycle), the
                       * staging slot 0x801D9724 is proven in-RAM-safe
                       * (R708 used it for the raw f15 copy), and
                       * xenolift_lzss_hle is proven (R703's c274 receipt:
                       * real coordinator code landed in window-0). Gates:
                       * ftab entry sane (LBA 150..300000, size
                       * 100000..200000 - f14 = 125304), LZSS header
                       * fingerprint exact, coordinator slot not already
                       * real code (0x8E020050 = byte 0x50), one-shot. On
                       * any decline the receipt names the gate that
                       * declined - no silent failure. */
                      static int r1005n;
                      /* R1007 (b8-c121): c121 proof - the full-handoff arm
                       * expanded at t=33s but bootmain's arena re-install
                       * (statestamp FULL HANDOFF #1-4 - the restart family)
                       * wiped the window before the t=63s knock, and the
                       * hard one-shot made the second entry-arm decline
                       * SILENTLY while the walk knocked TWICE (r31=80042370
                       * then 80077FC8 - module-window dispatch code). Re-fire
                       * on every empty-door knock: cap 4 re-expansions per
                       * process, each receipted, so a wiped door refills
                       * before the next knock lands. */
                      uint32_t ft_e = 0x800100ACu + 7u * 12u;   /* file#14 entry */
                      uint32_t ft_lba = xenolift_mem[ft_e - 0x80000000u]
                                      | ((uint32_t)xenolift_mem[ft_e - 0x80000000u + 1u] << 8)
                                      | ((uint32_t)xenolift_mem[ft_e - 0x80000000u + 2u] << 16);
                      uint32_t ft_sz = xenolift_mem[ft_e - 0x80000000u + 3u]
                                      | ((uint32_t)xenolift_mem[ft_e - 0x80000000u + 4u] << 8)
                                      | ((uint32_t)xenolift_mem[ft_e - 0x80000000u + 5u] << 16);
                      uint32_t co_w0 = xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u);
                      if (r1005n < 4
                          && ft_lba >= 150u && ft_lba < 300000u
                          && ft_sz >= 100000u && ft_sz <= 200000u
                          && (co_w0 & 0xFFu) != 0x50u) {
                          r1005n++;
                          uint32_t nsec = (ft_sz + 2047u) / 2048u;
                          if (nsec > 64u) nsec = 64u;
                          int ok = 1;
                          uint8_t *stg = xenolift_mem + (0x801D9724u - 0x80000000u);
                          for (uint32_t i = 0; i < nsec && ok; i++)
                              ok = (disc_read_lba(ft_lba + i, stg + 2048u * i) == 0);
                          uint32_t hdr = xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D9724u);
                          if (ok && (hdr & 0x00FFFFFFu) == 0x0003FAFEu) {
                              uint32_t w0 = hdr & 0x003FFFFFu;
                              int pr = xenolift_lzss_hle(0x801D9724u, 0x8006FAF0u, w0);
                              r861_out("[fldx2] R1005 RETARGET EXPANDED: file#14 LBA=%u size=%u staged %u sectors, LZSS %d of %u words -> coordinator slot now: %08X %08X %08X %08X\n",
                                      ft_lba, ft_sz, nsec, pr, w0,
                                      xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u),
                                      xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 4u),
                                      xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 8u),
                                      xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 12u));
                          } else {
                              r861_out("[fldx2] R1005 RETARGET DECLINED: ftab file#14 LBA=%u size=%u read=%s staged-hdr=%08X (want 00xxxx3FAFE) - gate named, no action\n",
                                      ft_lba, ft_sz, ok ? "ok" : "FAIL",
                                      hdr);
                          }
                      } else if (r1005n < 4) {
                          r1005n++;
                          r861_out("[fldx2] R1005 RETARGET GATE-DECLINED: ftab file#14 LBA=%u size=%u coord_w0=%08X (gates: LBA 150..300000, size 100000..200000, coord not-already-code 0x50)\n",
                                  ft_lba, ft_sz, co_w0);
                      }
                  }
                  {   /* R705 [fldreloc2] c276 PROOF: modtrace caught the kernel
                       * calling INTO the stage-2 slot 0x801D3000 (entries #1-11,
                       * caller 80019C0C) whose live words are ALL ZERO - not stale
                       * movie bytes; a nop-slide through zeros that ended in the
                       * computed-garbage fault (0x14E8FFFC). The kernel STAGED
                       * file-15 this very run (f15 bulk: 38 sectors -> heap-top
                       * 0x801F8724, 92180B, FDF8->0 @t=15s) exactly like it staged
                       * f14 @0x801D9724 before our R703 expansion into window-0.
                       * Movie-era precedent: the stage-2 slot holds the SECOND
                       * module's expanded code (movie called 0x801D3538 in it).
                       * The f15 expansion (LZSS staged->slot) is the install step
                       * our bypass skips - run it HERE, gated: staged header sane
                       * + slot still zero + one-shot. If the header is bogus we
                       * print it and DON'T expand (probe-only fallback). */
                      /* R1188 (c398/c399 receipts): RETIRED - the stale-f15-image class. Receipts: our own R797
 * comment (c380) already convicted this expansion - "the overlap region 0x801D3000+ gets
 * file17's real field-era code where our stale f15 image sat". R797's file17 expansion spans
 * 0x801C0000-0x801E5908, covering the whole stage-2 window; c399 head-history receipts the
 * sequence: R705 expands f15 at 29447, the cells read ZERO again by 29490 (R797 claiming its
 * region), the game's install fn 80041C64 writes, and the final mixed head (8C229D40 911032F3)
 * is what fn 800443A8 dispatched through to the c397 SIGSEGV. The stage-2 module is AOT-
 * translated since the overlay pivot - the guest-RAM copy is data, and stale f15 data there
 * clobbers file17's real content that the native code reads. Retire the pair (R708 below exists
 * solely to feed this expansion). */
static int r705n = 1; /* R1188: start spent - retired */
                      uint32_t _f15 = xenolift_mem_read32(0x801F8724u);
                      uint32_t _w0f = _f15 & 0x003FFFFFu;
                      /* R708: the staged f15 copy at heap-top OVERFLOWS RAM
                       * (0x801F8724 + 92180 > 0x80200000) so its tail was never
                       * in RAM - the HLE hit missing input mid-stream (the -1
                       * receipt, zeros past the head). Read the FULL f15 stream
                       * straight from the disc (LBA 108995, 46 sectors) into the
                       * consumed f14 staging slot 0x801D9724 (125KB, fully
                       * in-RAM) and expand from THERE. Raw copy also dumped to
                       * f15_raw.bin for offline format verification. */
                      {
                          static int r708_n = 1; /* R1188: start spent - retired with R705 (feeds only the stale expansion) */
                          if (r708_n < 1) {
                              r708_n++;
                              uint8_t sbuf[2048];
                              uint32_t got = 0;
                              for (uint32_t lba = 108995u; lba <= 109040u && got < 92180u; lba++) {
                                  if (disc_read_lba(lba, sbuf) != 0) break;
                                  uint32_t n = 92180u - got; if (n > 2048u) n = 2048u;
                                  memcpy(xenolift_mem + (0x801D9724u - 0x80000000u) + got, sbuf, n);
                                  got += n;
                              }
                              FILE *rf = fopen("f15_raw.bin", "wb");
                              if (rf) { fwrite(xenolift_mem + (0x801D9724u - 0x80000000u), 1, got, rf); fclose(rf); }
                              r861_out("[f15disc] R708 f15 read from DISC: %u bytes -> 0x801D9724 (staged-copy header was %08X, disc-copy header %08X), raw dump -> f15_raw.bin\n",
                                      got, _f15, xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D9724u));
                          }
                      }
                      uint32_t _src_f15 = 0x801D9724u; /* R708: expand from the complete disc copy */
                      uint32_t _h_f15 = xenolift_mem_read32(_src_f15);
                      _w0f = _h_f15 & 0x003FFFFFu; /* R708: authoritative header from the disc copy */
                      if (r705n < 1
                          && _w0f >= 0x0800u && _w0f <= 0x40000u   /* R706: f15 header 0002C0C6 (180678) = same size-encoding family as f14's 0003FAFE - gate widened */
                          && xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3000u) == 0u
                          && xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3004u) == 0u) {
                          int _prf = xenolift_lzss_hle(_src_f15, 0x801D3000u, _w0f);
                          r705n++;
                          r861_out("[fldreloc2] R705 f15 second-module EXPANDED into stage-2 slot: staged@801F8724 (header %08X) -> 0x801D3000, %d of %u words, slot now: %08X %08X %08X %08X\n",
                                  _f15, _prf, _w0f,
                                  xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3000u),
                                  xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3004u),
                                  xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3008u),
                                  xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D300Cu));
                          {   /* R733 [f15w0]: EVIDENCE (c304/c305): f15's own vtable
                               * head {5, 80072878, 80072898, 800728D8, ...} points
                               * INTO window-0 (0x8007xxxx) - absolute self-pointers
                               * target where the module truly lives. Our stage-2
                               * placement (0x801D3000) was the WRONG ROOM: the field
                               * second pass belongs in window-0, replacing the f14
                               * install. Expand f15 over window-0 (same src, the
                               * complete disc copy) so the kernel's state-1 call to
                               * 0x80077E88 finds the real coordinator code. Test-
                               * labeled: revertible one-shot, slot printed before/
                               * after so the digest PROVES what landed. */
                              static int r733n;
                              if (r733n < 1) {
                                  r733n++;
                                  uint32_t _b0 = xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u);
                                  /* R734: c306 - the disc copy @801D9724 FAILED the decode
                                   * (-1 bytes) while the kernel's own staged copy
                                   * @801F8724 decodes cleanly (R705 proof, 180422B) -
                                   * the copies differ past the header. Use the PROVEN
                                   * source and photograph both. */
                                  r861_out("[f15w0] R734 copies: disc@801D9724: %08X %08X %08X %08X | staged@801F8724: %08X %08X %08X %08X\n",
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D9724u), xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D9728u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D972Cu), xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D9730u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x801F8724u), xenolift_mem_read32(0x801F8728u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x801F872Cu), xenolift_mem_read32(0x801F8730u));
                                  int _prw = xenolift_lzss_hle(0x801F8724u, 0x8006FAF0u, _w0f);
                                  r861_out("[f15w0] R734 f15 EXPANDED into window-0 from STAGED copy (evidence: f15 vtable points 8007xxxx): %d bytes, coordinator slot 0x80077E88 before=%08X AFTER: %08X %08X %08X %08X\n",
                                          _prw, _b0,
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 4u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 8u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 12u));
                              }
                          }
                          {   /* R735 [f15w0]: ALL-CASES install (Jos c309: "look
                               * at sources for field module fix and do so in all
                               * cases"). Sources confirm the design (xenogears-
                               * recomp: field/battle/worldmap are overlay modules
                               * captured the moment they load). R705's stage-2
                               * decode is PROVEN (180,422B @0x801D3000, every
                               * cycle). Copy the decoded image over window-0
                               * directly - zero decode dependency, succeeds in
                               * ALL cases regardless of the R734 decode outcome
                               * (same bytes if it succeeded, clean overwrite of a
                               * half-written window if it failed). */
                              static int r735n;
                              if (r735n < 1) {
                                  r735n++;
                                  uint32_t _b0w = xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u);
                                  memcpy(xenolift_mem + (0x8006FAF0u - 0x80000000u),
                                         xenolift_mem + (0x801D3000u - 0x80000000u),
                                         _w0f);
                                  r861_out("[f15w0] R735 window-0 overwrite from PROVEN stage-2 image (%u bytes): slot before=%08X AFTER: %08X %08X %08X %08X\n",
                                          (unsigned)_w0f, _b0w,
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 4u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 8u),
                                          xenolift_mem_read32(0x80077E88u + 12u));
                              }
                          }
                          {   /* R707: arm the R493 terminator convention proactively -
                               * the 722KB f15 footprint overlays heap-record space; any
                               * walk reaching the region concludes at heap-top. */
                              xenolift_mem_write32(0x801FBFF8u, 0x801FC000u);
                              { uint32_t _t = xenolift_mem_read32(0x801FBFFCu);
                                if ((_t & 0x00200000u) == 0u) xenolift_mem_write32(0x801FBFFCu, _t | 0x00200000u); }
                              r861_out("[fldreloc2] R707 heap terminator armed at 0x801FBFF8/0x801FBFFC (walks conclude at heap-top)\n"); }

                      } else if (r705n < 1) {
                          r705n++;
                          r861_out("[fldreloc2] R705 f15 staged header NOT sane (%08X at 0x801F8724) or slot non-zero (%08X) - NOT expanding, evidence only\n",
                                  _f15, xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3000u));
                      }
                  }

    {   /* R797 [f17ins] (c380): FILE17 IS THE BAND WRITER. Evidence chain:
         * c378 jsrc = structured sweep of 0x801CAFF4-0x801D2F58 (all zeros,
         * guest executes empty lot); c380 f16hdr = file17 @LBA 109123 header
         * 0x00025908 = SAME LZSS family as f14 (0x0003FAFE) / f15 (0x0002C0C6)
         * -> 153,864 bytes expanded; EVERY observed walk target fits inside
         * [0x801C0000, 0x801C0000+0x25908) (0x801CAFF4=AFF4, 0x801D008C=
         * 1008C, 0x801D2F58=12F58, 0x801D3004=13004 - the "code at
         * 0x801D3004" site). The field-era second half is file17 expanded
         * at 0x801C0000; our movie-era f15 expansion at 0x801D3000 occupies
         * the wrong era's slot. Deliver file17 like R708 delivered f15:
         * stage raw at the CONSUMED f14 slot (0x801D9724), LZSS-expand to
         * 0x801C0000. The overlap region 0x801D3000+ gets file17's real
         * field-era code where our stale f15 image sat. */
        static int r797n;
        if (r797n < 1 && xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D2F58u) == 0u) {
            r797n++;
            uint32_t _lba = 109123u, _raw = 70944u, _exp = 0x25908u;
            uint32_t _stage = 0x801D9724u, _dst = 0x801C0000u;
            int ok = 1;
            uint8_t sec[2048];
            for (uint32_t so = 0; so < _raw && ok; so += 2048u) {
                if (disc_read_lba(_lba + so/2048u, sec) != 0) ok = 0;
                else memcpy(xenolift_mem + (_stage - 0x80000000u) + so, sec, 2048u);
            }
            uint32_t _h = xenolift_mem_read32(_stage);
            r861_out("[f17ins] R797 file17 staged @%08X: header %08X (want 00025908), %s\n",
                    _stage, _h, ok ? "read ok" : "DISC READ FAILED");
            if (ok && _h == 0x00025908u) {
                int _pr = xenolift_lzss_hle(_stage, _dst, _exp);
                r861_out("[f17ins] R797 file17 EXPANDED: %d of %u bytes -> 0x801C0000 (band now real field-era code; walk sites get their content)\n",
                        _pr, _exp);
                r861_out("[f17ins] R797 verify: base %08X %08X | 801CAFF4: %08X %08X | 801D008C: %08X %08X | 801D2F58: %08X %08X | 801D3004: %08X %08X\n",
                        xenolift_mem_read32(_dst), xenolift_mem_read32(_dst + 4u),
                        xenolift_mem_read32(0x801CAFF4u), xenolift_mem_read32(0x801CAFF4u + 4u),
                        xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D008Cu), xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D008Cu + 4u),
                        xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D2F58u), xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D2F58u + 4u),
                        xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3004u), xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3004u + 4u));
            } else if (ok) {
                r861_out("[f17ins] R797 header MISMATCH - NOT expanding (want 00025908)\n");
            }
        }
    }
    {   /* R796 [f16hdr]: the second-half band 0x801C0000-0x801D3000 is
         * ALL ZEROS on our path (c379 walkmap census) - the module's
         * lower region never gets written. The kernel looked up files
         * 16/17/18 (field-era family, first-ever lookups earlier cycles).
         * Dump their disc headers + LZSS size-decode so the next cycle can
         * deliver the missing file to the band like R708 did for f15. */
        static int r796n;
        if (r796n < 1) {
            r796n++;
            static const struct { uint32_t lba; const char *tag; } files[3] = {
                {109041u, "file16"}, {109123u, "file17"}, {109158u, "file18"}};
            for (int fi = 0; fi < 3; fi++) {
                uint8_t hbuf[2048];
                if (disc_read_lba(files[fi].lba, hbuf) != 0) {
                    r861_out("[f16hdr] R796 %s @LBA %u: DISC READ FAILED\n", files[fi].tag, files[fi].lba);
                    continue;
                }
                uint32_t w0 = (uint32_t)hbuf[0] | ((uint32_t)hbuf[1] << 8) | ((uint32_t)hbuf[2] << 16) | ((uint32_t)hbuf[3] << 24);
                uint32_t sz = w0 & 0x003FFFFFu;
                r861_out("[f16hdr] R796 %s @LBA %u: head=%08X size-bits=%u (LZSS-family gate 0x0800-0x40000: %s) words:",
                        files[fi].tag, files[fi].lba, w0, sz,
                        (sz >= 0x0800u && sz <= 0x80000u) ? "YES" : "no",
                        sz);
                for (int i = 1; i <= 7; i++) {
                    uint32_t w = (uint32_t)hbuf[4*i] | ((uint32_t)hbuf[4*i+1] << 8) | ((uint32_t)hbuf[4*i+2] << 16) | ((uint32_t)hbuf[4*i+3] << 24);
                    r861_out(" %08X", w);
                }
                r861_out("\n");
            }
        }
    }
    {   /* R792 [misalign] (c375): the walk calls module methods with
         * caller r31=0x801D3008 (modfn #1-2: the guest EXECUTES at
         * 0x801D3004), but our expanded image holds a VTABLE at
         * 0x801D3000 (00000005 80072878 80072898 ...). The real layout
         * has CODE there: the call site at 0x801D3004 must be a jal to
         * fn_8007800C = word 0x0C01E003. If our image is the right
         * module placed at the wrong base, that jal word exists at some
         * image offset - the offset gives the TRUE base
         * (0x801D3004 - byte_offset). Scan for the jal encodings of the
         * two methods the walk actually calls (fn_8007800C = 0x0C01E003,
         * fn_80078054 = 0x0C01E015) plus the vtable-triple recurrence. */
        static int r792n;
        if (r792n < 2) {
            r792n++;
            uint32_t hits_a = 0, off_a = 0, hits_b = 0, off_b = 0;
            for (uint32_t w = 0; w < (135168u >> 2); w++) {
                uint32_t v = xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3000u + 4u*w);
                if (v == 0x0C01E003u) { hits_a++; if (!off_a) off_a = w; }
                if (v == 0x0C01E015u) { hits_b++; if (!off_b) off_b = w; }
            }
            r861_out("[misalign] R792 scan: jal fn_8007800C hits=%u first word-off=%u (byte %u => implied true base %08X); jal fn_80078054 hits=%u first word-off=%u (byte %u => implied base %08X)\n",
                    hits_a, off_a, off_a*4, 0x801D3004u - off_a*4,
                    hits_b, off_b, off_b*4, 0x801D3004u - off_b*4);
            uint32_t triple_off = 0;
            for (uint32_t w = 1; w < ((135168u >> 2) - 2); w++) {
                if (xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3000u + 4u*w) == 0x80072878u
                    && xenolift_mem_read32(0x801D3000u + 4u*(w+1)) == 0x80072898u) { triple_off = w; break; }
            }
            r861_out("[misalign] R792 vtable-triple (80072878/80072898) recurrence: first word-off %u beyond the image head\n", triple_off);
        }
    }
}
